    background: `The Admiral's Anchorage is a registered historic 180-room landmark hotel overlooking the historic colonial harbor of a historic seaport city in New England. Constructed in 1892 as a bustling maritime exchange and grand transit hotel, the property retains magnificent architectural features, including hand-carved mahogany archways, maritime brass fixtures, and a 130-year-old nautical map library. The hotel operates with an ADR of $310 and an annual occupancy rate of 68%.

While mainstream commercial travelers occasionally book the property due to its central downtown location, guest exit surveys indicate that many guests view the historic quirks—such as historic cage elevators, varied room layouts, and lack of modern open-concept suites—as inconveniences rather than distinctive assets. Competing against newly built glass-and-steel chain hotels located along the highway ring that boast identical, generic modern rooms and indoor pools, the Admiral's Anchorage has struggled to articulate why its rich heritage warrants a rate premium.

Concurrently, nationwide travel trends demonstrate that the "Cultural Heritage Traveler" segment represents one of the most lucrative demographics in the United States. According to the National Trust for Historic Preservation, cultural heritage travelers stay an average of 4.8 nights (compared to 3.2 nights for standard leisure travelers), spend 38% more per day on local dining, artisan crafts, and museum admissions, and consistently prioritize lodging properties that reflect authentic sense-of-place.

The hotel's ownership board, in conjunction with the Municipal Historical Society, has approved a $90,000 marketing campaign titled "Living History on the Harbor." The objective is to reposition the Admiral's Anchorage as an active historic maritime museum and living historical treasure where guests do not simply sleep, but step into authentic colonial and 19th-century maritime history.

You and your partner (serving as the Cultural Tourism Marketing Director and Community Engagement Manager) have been requested to present your campaign to the Managing Trustee and Historical Preservation Board Executive (played by the judge). You must present a multi-faceted heritage marketing blueprint. Your presentation must detail museum partnerships, historical room storytelling audio tours, curated historical lecture-and-dine evenings, targeted promotion to national history societies, and heritage packaging that establishes the hotel as an indispensable cultural destination.`,
